{:edb/db-before-t 871 :edb/db-after-t 872 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534184 50 #inst "2026-10-06T08:46:46.518Z" 13194139534184 true] [17592186059600 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281107 13194139534184 true] [17592186068825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281128 13194139534184 true] [17592186069290 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534184 true] [17592186069290 1100 "Select the function that is continuous at $x = 2$." 13194139534184 false] [17592186069290 1100 "Select the function that is continuous at $x=2.$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186069290 1145 "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper." 13194139534184 true] [17592186083223 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281163 13194139534184 true] [17592186087783 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281114 13194139534184 true] [17592186096553 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281149 13194139534184 true] [17592186096760 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281142 13194139534184 true] [17592186110684 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281121 13194139534184 true] [17592186115139 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281100 13194139534184 true] [17592186124390 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281135 13194139534184 true] [17592186129080 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281156 13194139534184 true] [17592186281100 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281101 13194139534184 true] [17592186281100 1089 "q-100462" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281100 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534184 true] [17592186281100 1100 "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{d-4}={243}^{d+3}.$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281100 1113 #uuid "428d9b88-f269-5db0-b633-82fc3de5cd40" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281100 1145 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={3}^{2}$ and $243={3}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{d-4}={({3}^{5})}^{d+3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(d-4)}={3}^{5(d+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-4) & =5(d+3) \\\\ 2d-8 & =5d+15 \\\\ -3d & =23 \\\\ d & =-\\frac{23}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281102 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281103 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281104 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281105 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281106 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1041 "selection" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281105 13194139534184 true] [17592186281101 1110 #uuid "b1550654-810d-5e1f-849f-6765df10bb45" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281102 1034 "-\\frac{20}{3}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281102 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281102 1111 #uuid "591788d1-f55f-5720-a917-a7d70d7b5c49" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281103 1034 "\\frac{21}{2}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281103 1111 #uuid "7f2fc541-889b-5126-a2e0-5eccf7ce18c2" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281104 1034 "-\\frac{3}{23}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281104 1111 #uuid "f34da75e-efc6-5e18-a5dc-3f37489a6b13" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281105 1034 "-\\frac{23}{3}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281105 1111 #uuid "1c69502c-a2d2-5be5-87a6-841219cdbb9b" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281106 1034 "\\frac{3}{32}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281106 1111 #uuid "679e44f2-6f95-57aa-8ea8-ef6b97091896" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281107 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281108 13194139534184 true] [17592186281107 1089 "q-103081" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281107 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534184 true] [17592186281107 1100 "The derivative of $y=x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281107 1113 #uuid "2fcf27aa-bd7f-5c4c-a133-df0a431c2288" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281107 1145 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =1-{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-(\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281109 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281110 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281111 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281112 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281113 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1041 "selection" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281113 13194139534184 true] [17592186281108 1110 #uuid "3f4345a0-a8a9-5108-a6f9-13c49a8fb52a" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281109 1034 "3" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281109 1111 #uuid "0094fe7a-2467-56c2-91e7-8ff8ac19e1b6" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281110 1034 "1" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281110 1111 #uuid "2223165d-c8f4-5f7c-91e1-20151475dd06" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281111 1034 "-3" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281111 1111 #uuid "f9cf0673-0755-5110-a06e-2880b9a5a3fe" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281112 1034 "1-\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281112 1111 #uuid "d5d3b65e-58f0-5d2f-ad11-3577ed9bf557" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281113 1034 "-1" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281113 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281113 1111 #uuid "feb9199d-6913-5e67-9a46-d69235f601cc" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281114 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281115 13194139534184 true] [17592186281114 1089 "q-113525" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281114 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534184 true] [17592186281114 1100 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x}.$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281114 1113 #uuid "005baec8-159c-5d30-8dfd-3282724abca2" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281114 1145 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+7x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x({x}^{2}+x+7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx({x}^{2}+x+7)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+x+7=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-4(1)(7) \\\\  & =1-28 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=0.$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281116 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281117 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281118 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281119 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281120 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1041 "selection" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281117 13194139534184 true] [17592186281115 1110 #uuid "dd217fc8-7bd7-5eb5-af5b-5d3429783636" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281116 1034 "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281116 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534184 true] [17592186281116 1111 #uuid "066962bf-e4c3-5c83-8151-ef8714e5e95d" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281117 1034 "$x=0$ only" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281117 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534184 true] [17592186281117 1111 #uuid "cf6718f4-b45b-5819-a3bc-95f484229d65" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281118 1034 "$x=1$ only" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534184 true] [17592186281118 1111 #uuid "7f597759-46b5-530c-bcd9-fa9268686357" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281119 1034 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534184 true] [17592186281119 1111 #uuid "accb6e9c-83c3-578f-8890-d64898fedbd7" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281120 1034 "$x=-7$ and $x=0$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534184 true] [17592186281120 1111 #uuid "d267a02a-abe1-503c-ac35-002d18aa2dfb" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281121 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281122 13194139534184 true] [17592186281121 1089 "q-124796" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281121 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534184 true] [17592186281121 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/assets/ee92f92a52d8ade2052a62a5c19906cd86027b47f22ec986a740b9379e66d8d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281121 1113 #uuid "269edd72-a80e-50b6-ad68-325c29bb6c28" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281121 1145 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{8})}=3\\sqrt{7}.\n$$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281123 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281124 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281125 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281126 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281127 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1041 "selection" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281125 13194139534184 true] [17592186281122 1110 #uuid "8595e1ea-eae2-597b-952f-73c46d410adb" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281123 1034 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281123 1111 #uuid "5fd1702c-6e01-5754-8271-ac7b89851be4" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281124 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281124 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281124 1111 #uuid "cb9996e0-19a6-5d01-9fdb-764b260469e5" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281125 1034 "3\\sqrt{7}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281125 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281125 1111 #uuid "8646fe34-fa28-5a36-8697-5ea5f3e9e3a5" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281126 1034 "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281126 1111 #uuid "f1797d55-6f7d-5505-9f22-ec5217f35478" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281127 1034 "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534184 true] [17592186281127 1111 #uuid "bfd045df-22da-5b4b-9b1e-ae1f6df8aa46" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281128 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281129 13194139534184 true] [17592186281128 1089 "q-27442" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281128 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534184 true] [17592186281128 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980518/assets/56433bbb908fde0c0a46f68665b9ac22360c00978238124b6fac9022576b3620.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c.$ What is the value of $c?$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281128 1113 #uuid "a413ad4d-6933-5f9a-b496-2be070e4b6bb" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281128 1145 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=3.$ Therefore, $c=3.$" 13194139534184 true] [17592186281129